  "title": "Oracle lacks L2 sequencer uptime validation enabling price manipulation during downtime",
  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\n\nThe Oracle extension records price snapshots through hooks that fire on every swap and position update in the Ekubo protocol. These snapshots form the foundation for all time-weighted average price calculations consumed by external protocols. However, the snapshot insertion logic completely ignores Layer 2 sequencer status, which creates a critical vulnerability if Ekubo deploys on any L2 chain.\n\nThe README explicitly states Ekubo is a \"multi-chain ownerless and permissionless contract\" designed for the EVM. There's a reasonable expectation this code will run on chains with centralized sequencers. Yet the oracle has zero awareness of sequencer uptime.\n\nHere's the core snapshot insertion function that gets called on every swap and position update:\n\n```solidity\nfunction maybeInsertSnapshot(PoolId poolId, address token) private {\n    unchecked {\n        Counts c;\n        assembly (\"memory-safe\") {\n            c := sload(token)\n        }\n\n        uint32 timePassed = uint32(block.timestamp) - c.lastTimestamp();\n        if (timePassed == 0) return;\n\n        uint32 index = c.index();\n\n        // we know count is always g.t. 0 in the places this is called\n        Snapshot last;\n        assembly (\"memory-safe\") {\n            last := sload(or(shl(32, token), index))\n        }\n\n        PoolState state = CORE.poolState(poolId);\n\n        uint128 liquidity = state.liquidity();\n        uint256 nonZeroLiquidity;\n        assembly (\"memory-safe\") {\n            nonZeroLiquidity := add(liquidity, iszero(liquidity))\n        }\n\n        Snapshot snapshot = createSnapshot({\n            _timestamp: uint32(block.timestamp),\n            _secondsPerLiquidityCumulative: last.secondsPerLiquidityCumulative()\n                + uint160(FixedPointMathLib.rawDiv(uint256(timePassed) << 128, nonZeroLiquidity)),\n            _tickCumulative: last.tickCumulative() + int64(uint64(timePassed)) * state.tick()\n        });\n        \n        // ... <store snapshot> ...\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe function calculates `timePassed` using `block.timestamp`, creates cumulative values based on that time delta, and stores the snapshot. On L2s with centralized sequencers, this logic breaks down catastrophically during sequencer downtime.\n\nAttack scenario in detail:\n\n**Phase 1: Sequencer Goes Down**\n\nWhen an L2 sequencer stops producing blocks, `block.timestamp` freezes. No new transactions are processed, but the real-world clock keeps ticking. This can happen due to:\n- Infrastructure failures\n- Maintenance windows  \n- Malicious attacks on sequencer infrastructure\n- Network partitions\n\nDuring this period, which can last minutes to hours, market prices continue to move on centralized\n\n**Phase 2: Sequencer Restart**\n\nWhen the sequencer comes back online, it starts processing the backlog of pending transactions. All these transactions execute with the same stale `block.timestamp` from when the sequencer went down. \n\nBut, the oracle sees this as normal activity. The first swap that executes calls `maybeInsertSnapshot()`, which calculates:\n\n```solidity\nuint32 timePassed = uint32(block.timestamp) - c.lastTimestamp();\n```\n\nIf the sequencer was down for an hour but `block.timestamp` only advanced by seconds (because it was frozen), `timePassed` is artificially small. The snapshot gets written with cumulative values that don't reflect the real time gap.\n\nWorse, trades execute at prices that may have been manipulated during the accumulation phase while the sequencer was down. These corrupted prices get locked into the oracle's TWAP history.\n\n**Phase 3: Grace Period Exploitation**\n\nEven after the sequencer is back up and `block.timestamp` starts advancing normally, there's a window where the oracle data is still contaminated. Attackers can:\n\n1. Execute trades during the first few blocks after restart at manipulated prices\n2. Have those trades recorded in oracle snapshots \n3. Wait for the TWAP window to incorporate those snapshots\n4. Exploit protocols that depend on the now-corrupted TWAP\n\n\n**Why This Matters More Than View Function Issues**\n\nTthis vulnerability strikes at the core of how the oracle collects data. The `maybeInsertSnapshot()` function isn't a helper for integrators - it's called directly by the protocol's `beforeSwap` and `beforeUpdatePosition` hooks:\n\n```solidity\nfunction beforeSwap(Locker, PoolKey memory poolKey, SwapParameters params)\n    external\n    override(BaseExtension, IExtension)\n    onlyCore\n{\n    if (params.amount() != 0) {\n        maybeInsertSnapshot(poolKey.toPoolId(), poolKey.token1);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nEvery swap that occurs writes a snapshot. If those snapshots are corrupted during sequencer downtime, the corruption is permanent and affects all downstream TWAP consumers forever (or until enough new snapshots cycle through the circular buffer).\n\n**Impact Assessment**\n\nThe impact depends on deployment context:\n- **On Ethereum mainnet**: No impact (no sequencer)\n- **On any L2**: Critical vulnerability enabling:\n  - Price manipulation through sequencer downtime windows\n  - Permanent corruption of oracle data\n  - Exploitation of all protocols depending on the oracle\n  - Loss of funds from liquidations/trades based on corrupted prices\n\nGiven the protocol is explicitly designed as \"multi-chain\" and most new deployments target L2s, this is a material risk worth addressing.\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\n\nImplement sequencer uptime validation:\n\n**1. Add sequencer uptime feed integration:**\n\n```solidity\n// Add to Oracle.sol state\naddress public immutable SEQUENCER_UPTIME_FEED;\nuint256 public constant GRACE_PERIOD_TIME = 3600; // 1 hour\n\nconstructor(ICore core, address sequencerFeed) BaseExtension(core) {\n    SEQUENCER_UPTIME_FEED = sequencerFeed;\n}\n```\n\n**2. Validate sequencer status before recording snapshots:**\n\n```solidity\nfunction maybeInsertSnapshot(PoolId poolId, address token) private {\n    // Skip sequencer check if feed not configured (mainnet deployment)\n    if (SEQUENCER_UPTIME_FEED != address(0)) {\n        (, int256 answer, uint256 startedAt,,) = \n            AggregatorV3Interface(SEQUENCER_UPTIME_FEED).latestRoundData();\n\n        // Check sequencer is up (answer == 0 means up for Chainlink feeds)\n        bool isSequencerUp = answer == 0;\n        if (!isSequencerUp) {\n            revert SequencerDown();\n        }\n\n        // Enforce grace period after sequencer restarts\n        // This prevents exploitation during the first blocks after restart\n        uint256 timeSinceUp = block.timestamp - startedAt;\n        if (timeSinceUp < GRACE_PERIOD_TIME) {\n            revert GracePeriodNotOver(timeSinceUp, GRACE_PERIOD_TIME);\n        }\n    }\n\n    unchecked {\n        Counts c;\n        assembly (\"memory-safe\") {\n            c := sload(token)\n        }\n\n        uint32 timePassed = uint32(block.timestamp) - c.lastTimestamp();\n        if (timePassed == 0) return;\n\n        // ... rest of snapshot logic ...\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**3. Add error definitions:**\n\n```solidity\nerror SequencerDown();\nerror GracePeriodNotOver(uint256 timeSinceUp, uint256 required);\n```\n\n**4. Deployment configuration:**\n\nFor mainnet deployments, pass `address(0)` as the sequencer feed to skip checks. For L2 deployments, use the appropriate Chainlink sequencer uptime feed:\n\n- Optimism: 0x371EAD81c9102C9BF4874A9075FFFf170F2Ee389\n- Arbitrum: 0xFdB631F5EE196F0ed6FAa767959853A9F217697D  \n- Base: 0xBCF85224fc0756B9Fa45aA7892530B47e10b6433\n\n**Alternative approach if Chainlink feeds unavailable:**\n\nIf deploying to a chain without Chainlink sequencer feeds, implement a simple heartbeat mechanism:\n\n```solidity\nuint256 public lastHeartbeat;\nuint256 public constant MAX_TIME_SINCE_HEARTBEAT = 300; // 5 minutes\n\nfunction updateHeartbeat() external {\n    lastHeartbeat = block.timestamp;\n}\n\nfunction maybeInsertSnapshot(PoolId poolId, address token) private {\n    // Ensure heartbeat is recent\n    if (block.timestamp - lastHeartbeat > MAX_TIME_SINCE_HEARTBEAT) {\n        revert StaleHeartbeat();\n    }\n    \n    // ... rest of logic ...\n}\n```\n\nThis requires an off-chain keeper to call `updateHeartbeat()` regularly, but provides basic protection against stale timestamps.\n\nThe critical point is that any L2 deployment needs some mechanism to detect and handle sequencer downtime. The oracle cannot blindly trust `block.timestamp` on chains with centralized sequencers.",
